      "independentReasons": [
        "The same-book definitions explicitly make angle sides rays and allow either region bounded by them, including the nonconvex region. Original and current 335(1) constrain M, not the constructed point, and do not specify convexity. The new construction selects the smaller side angle beta only to define coordinates, without requiring M or X to lie inside it.",
        "For noncollinear sides w1=c*u+k*v and w2=c*u-k*v, c,k>0, squared ray distances are |X|^2-max(0,X dot wi)^2. Their equality gives exactly b={t*u:t>=0} union K={t*u+z*v:c*t+k*|z|<=0}. Equal positive projections give the forward bisector; both nonpositive projections give K; one strictly positive and one nonpositive cannot give equality. Thus the locus applies independently of which source-defined angular region contains M.",
        "Q is actually constructible, not an oracle: if M lies in the closed cone K, take M. Otherwise a closest point cannot lie in K's interior, since moving toward M would decrease its distance. On each boundary ray the closest point is its perpendicular foot if forward, otherwise O. Comparing these two distances yields the closest Q in K. The boundary rays are constructible from perpendiculars to the given sides; perpendicular feet, distance comparisons and the parallel through Q are ordinary straightedge-and-compass steps.",
        "For every lambda>=0, Q-lambda*u remains in K since its defining inequality decreases by lambda*c. Projection minimality also gives (M-Q) dot u>=0: Q-lambda*u is feasible, so (M-Q) dot (-u)<=0. Hence |Q-lambda*u-M|^2=|MQ|^2+2*lambda*(M-Q) dot u+lambda^2 increases strictly for positive lambda and is unbounded. At r=MQ choose Q; at r>MQ the circle intersects the constructed backward ray, supplying one valid X. If Q=M, this reduces to choosing lambda=r.",
        "First taking any circle intersection on the closed forward ray is sufficient. If it has none, the cone construction succeeds exactly when r>=d(M,K). Each component is connected and unbounded, so its attainable distances from M range from its minimum to infinity. Thus existence for r>=0 is exactly r>=min(d(M,b),d(M,K)); r=0 works exactly when M belongs to the union. No interior constraint or full-locus enumeration is needed.",
        "The former convex-M formulas and counts remain expressly qualified; their source-domain exclusion and mandatory approval demand are replaced by the general construction. Conditional opposite-ray and coincident-ray statements are mathematically correct conditions, not claims that the source supplied degenerate angles. No new source fact or human gate is inferred."
      ],
